(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-venous-disorders-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Venous Disorders Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Within what interval should superficial thrombophlebitis be reexamined?","choices":["3 months","7 to 10 days","24 hours","3 to 4 weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A repeat physical examination within 7 to 10 days assesses whether the condition is resolving or progressing, with repeat duplex ultrasound if signs persist or worsen. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the most common predisposing factor for varicose veins?","choices":["Pregnancy","Family history","Prolonged standing","Heavy lifting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Family history is the most common predisposing factor for developing varicose veins; periods of high venous pressure and pregnancy also contribute. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which finding makes outpatient therapy for deep vein thrombosis inappropriate?","choices":["Reliable follow-up","Normal kidney function","Iliofemoral (proximal) clot","Stable vital signs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient therapy is not appropriate for iliofemoral proximal clots, concurrent symptomatic pulmonary embolism, high bleeding risk or comorbidities needing inpatient care. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Where does the itching of chronic venous disease often begin?","choices":["Around the lateral malleolus","Over the dorsum of the foot","Behind the knee in the popliteal fossa","Around the medial malleolus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Itching of the lower legs often starts around the medial malleolus, the inner ankle; arterial disease tends to affect the lateral side, so a medial start points toward a venous cause. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How reliable is the Homan sign (calf pain when the foot is flexed upward) for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Both sensitive and specific","Neither sensitive nor specific","Sensitive but not specific","Specific but not sensitive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive Homan sign can occur in deep vein thrombosis, but it is neither sensitive nor specific, so it can neither rule the diagnosis in nor out. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon without which history?","choices":["Recent long-haul air travel","Estrogen-containing contraception","Advanced chronic kidney disease","Venous cannulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon in the absence of a history of venous cannulation, such as venipuncture or catheterization, which gives infection a route into the vein. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"How many points does an alternative diagnosis at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis subtract in the Wells criteria?","choices":["Three points","Five points","Two points","One point"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An alternative diagnosis at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis scores minus 2, the only negative item; every other item adds 1 point. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which age group is listed among the risk factors for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Older than 60 years","Younger than 20 years","Younger than 30 years","Aged 20 to 40 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Age above 60 years is listed among the other risk factors for deep vein thrombosis, alongside overweight, heart failure, cancer, chronic kidney disease and a sedentary lifestyle. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"After parenteral therapy for 5 days, which agents can anticoagulation be switched to?","choices":["Rivaroxaban or apixaban","Warfarin or heparin","Aspirin or clopidogrel","Dabigatran or edoxaban"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the second strategy, parenteral therapy is switched after 5 days to a novel anticoagulant: dabigatran, a direct thrombin inhibitor, or edoxaban, an anti-Xa agent. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Pulmonary embolism complicates up to what share of upper extremity deep vein thromboses?","choices":["15% to 30%","40% to 50%","Almost never","Up to 6%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary embolism occurs in up to 6% of upper extremity clots, compared with 15% to 30% of lower extremity clots, so the leg carries far greater embolic risk. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the primary presenting symptom of chronic venous insufficiency?","choices":["Cool, pale toes","Numbness across the foot","Progressive pitting edema","Calf pain when walking"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Progressive pitting edema of the leg, usually the lower leg, is the primary presenting symptom, typically with dull discomfort that worsens on standing. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which history typically accompanies lymphedema when it mimics deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["A recent long flight","Starting a new blood pressure drug","A sudden twisting injury","Pelvic surgery or radiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphedema is chronic edema of the extremities, usually with a history of pelvic surgery, malignancy or radiation therapy that damaged lymphatic drainage. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Without treatment, what is the cumulative rate of fatal pulmonary embolism in symptomatic deep vein thrombosis without pulmonary embolism?","choices":["Under 1%","About 3%","About 9%","About 25%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Untreated, the cumulative rate of fatal pulmonary embolism is under 1% in symptomatic deep vein thrombosis without embolism, rising to 3% with a nonmassive embolism. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What defines superficial phlebitis?","choices":["Vein wall inflammation without a clot","Vein wall inflammation with a clot","Infected vein wall with pus","Clot within a deep leg vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Superficial phlebitis is inflammation of the wall of a vein in the absence of thrombus; when a clot is also present the condition is called superficial thrombophlebitis, and duplex ultrasound shows the difference (wall thickening alone versus thickening with clot). — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"In the Wells criteria, calf swelling counts when it exceeds what difference from the other leg?","choices":["More than 10 cm","More than 3 cm","More than 1 cm","More than 5 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calf swelling scores when it is more than 3 cm larger than the other leg, measured 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity, which standardizes where the calf is measured. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Under the Virchow triad, cancer belongs to which component?","choices":["Venous stasis","Endothelial trauma","None of the three","Hypercoagulable state"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cancer sits in the hypercoagulable component of the Virchow triad, alongside oral contraceptives, postmenopausal estrogen therapy and pregnancy, all of which tip blood toward clotting. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which component of the Virchow triad includes postoperative inactivity?","choices":["Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","Turbulent arterial flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immobilization, such as prolonged sitting or postoperative inactivity, slows venous flow, so it falls in the stasis component of the Virchow triad; immobilization raises risk about 20-fold. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"For which clot may thrombolysis be considered in deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["A large proximal clot","An isolated calf clot","A superficial vein clot","Any clot with active bleeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thrombolysis, in which tissue plasminogen activator breaks down the clot, is considered for a large proximal deep vein thrombosis or a stable pulmonary embolism. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"For how long after delivery does postpartum status raise deep vein thrombosis risk?","choices":["The first 6 to 12 weeks","The first 2 weeks only","The first 6 months","The first 2 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The first 6 to 12 weeks postpartum is a listed risk factor for deep vein thrombosis, and pregnancy itself adds a hypercoagulable state and pelvic vein involvement. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Advancing age raises deep vein thrombosis risk above which age?","choices":["50 years","75 years","60 years","30 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age over 60 years is listed among the other risk factors for deep vein thrombosis, alongside obesity, smoking, heart failure and a personal or family history of clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the purpose of emollients in venous skin care?","choices":["Shrink the dilated veins","Dissolve deeper skin scarring","Prevent new clot formation","Maintain the skin barrier"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emollients maintain the skin barrier, preventing dryness and fissuring and reducing scratching, which lowers the risk of skin breakdown in chronic venous insufficiency. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How is an arteriovenous fistula of the extremity diagnosed?","choices":["By skin punch biopsy","By ankle-brachial index","By physical examination","By D-dimer measurement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis is determined by physical examination; computed tomography angiography and angiography can then confirm it and give the location and size of the fistula. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which inherited hypercoagulable state is listed among the Virchow triad risk factors?","choices":["Estrogen replacement","Factor V Leiden","Cancer","Pregnancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Factor V Leiden is the most common inherited hypercoagulable state and heads the list, which also names prothrombin gene mutation and protein C, protein S and antithrombin deficiency. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Where should a patient with a venous ulcer be referred for care?","choices":["A pain management clinic","Wound care management","A hematology clinic","A rheumatology clinic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Venous ulcers are referred to wound care management, because they will not heal unless edema is controlled and compression is applied, and healing can take months. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"An elevated D-dimer implies increased activity of which process?","choices":["Vitamin K depletion","Red cell destruction","Fibrinolysis","Platelet production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An elevated D-dimer implies increased fibrinolysis, the breakdown of fibrin clot; it can occur in a deep vein thrombosis but also in many other conditions. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"For superficial thrombophlebitis, which situation prompts adding anticoagulation?","choices":["Pain relieved by warm compresses","Extensive clot or higher thrombosis risk","Any tender palpable cord in a leg","Purulent drainage over the vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation may be started when clot burden is extensive or the patient is at intermediate or higher risk of thrombosis; otherwise warm compresses and anti-inflammatories are enough. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"How is the D-dimer characterized as a test for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Both sensitive and specific","Neither sensitive nor specific","Sensitive but not specific","Specific but not sensitive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is sensitive but not specific: a negative result is reassuring, while a positive result may reflect fibrinolysis from many causes rather than a deep vein thrombosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"In the low likelihood Wells category, what happens after a negative D-dimer?","choices":["The test is repeated in a week","The work-up stops","Duplex ultrasound follows","Venography is performed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the low likelihood Wells category a negative D-dimer ends the work-up, since the test is sensitive enough to rule out deep vein thrombosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"After a negative thigh ultrasound in a high likelihood patient, which follow-up step is appropriate?","choices":["Repeat thigh ultrasound in a week","Discharge with no follow-up","Immediate thrombolysis","Inferior vena cava filter placement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A negative thigh ultrasound in a high likelihood patient does not end the work-up: lower leg ultrasound, phlebography or a repeat thigh ultrasound in a week can follow. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What share of all deep vein thromboses occur in the upper extremity?","choices":["5% to 10%","25% to 30%","40% to 50%","Under 1%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Upper extremity clots make up only 5% to 10% of all deep vein thromboses, although the incidence is rising each year; the lower extremity accounts for the great majority. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
